Z||000006||mrfiero||01-11-2000||04:28 PM||||ChadMan,<P>Colorado has what is known as the "make my day" law.  Basically you, as a homeowner, etc. have the right to defend yourself and your property with whatever force is necessary.  Of course it's just inside your home...i.e. you're asleep and a burglar breaks into your house......you can legally shoot him dead.  Now if you woke up and he ran out the door and was trying to get away and you shot him you would have some explaining to do.  There are some gray areas with this that I'm not too sure about, but if someone breaks into your house and you shoot him while he is inside your house and you feared for your safety/well being then the shooting was justifiable and you wouldn't be held criminally liable. <P>I believe that is how it works (I've never had to find out....thank goodness!)||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||01-12-2000||01:48 PM||||I think most (if not all) legal systems have a concept of self defense. The problem is that it usually gets real "gray" on whether or not someone really was in danger and if they could have used less force. <P>The really scary part is that whether or not they were justified does not seem to be based on the few facts they were aware of in the few seconds they had to make the decision. But rather on all the information and possibilities that come up later.<P>Even if they avoid criminal charges they are still likely to wind up in a civil suit. Even in a state where they were legally justified, a civil suit can still be filed. A jury could find someone financially liable for shooting a criminal based on the criminal's testimony that he had no intention of harming them. Or even worse, based on the emotional testimony of a deceased criminal's family that he was "a nice guy" and could not have meant them any harm.<P>Whatever happened to common sense...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
